FROTZ(6)	    UNIX Programmer's Manual		 FROTZ(6)



NAME
     frotz - interpreter for Infocom and other Z-machine story files

SYNOPSIS
     frotz [ _o_p_t_i_o_n_s ] _s_t_o_r_y_-_f_i_l_e

DESCRIPTION
     _F_r_o_t_z plays a Z-machine _s_t_o_r_y_ _f_i_l_e -- the compiled form
     Infocom shipped Zork and its kin in, and the one modern authoring
     systems such as Inform still target.  Story versions 1 through 8
     are understood.

     The screen is full-screen, not teletype: a status line (versions 1
     to 3) or a split upper window (version 4 and later) sits above a
     scrolling lower window; reverse video, bold and underline render on
     the Super-Hi-Res console, and version 5 and later may use its eight
     colours.  The whole story -- which for later versions far exceeds a
     single 64 KB bank -- is loaded into one bank-spanning Memory-Manager
     block, so any game the machine has room for will run.

     Save and restore use the portable Quetzal format, so a saved game
     moves between _f_r_o_t_z and other Standard interpreters.

OPTIONS
     -h _N   Set the screen height to _N rows.  The default is the
           console's own size.

     -w _N   Set the screen width to _N columns.

     -m    Do not pause with a ***MORE*** prompt when the screen fills.
           Use it when input is coming from a file rather than the
           keyboard, so the game never blocks waiting for a keypress.

     -p    Plain ASCII output: render accented (Latin-1) characters as
           their nearest plain-ASCII spelling.

     -s _N   Seed the random number generator with _N, for repeatable
           play.  Without it the seed comes from the clock.

     -u _N   Keep _N levels of undo (0 disables the undo verb).

     -x    Expand the single-letter abbreviations g, x and z to the
           words again, examine and wait.

     -Z _N   Error-reporting mode: 0 reports nothing, 1 the first error,
           2 every error, 3 exits on any error.

     The debugging switches -a and -A (watch attribute set/test) and -o
     and -O (watch object movement/location) trace the running game and
     are of interest mainly to authors.

PLAYING
     Type commands to the game at its prompt.  While a game runs, a line
     that begins with a backslash is read by the interpreter rather than
     the game; _\_\_h_e_l_p lists those runtime commands.

     The save and restore verbs prompt for a filename and write or read
     a Quetzal save file in the current directory.  With undo enabled
     (see -u), the undo verb steps back one turn.

FILES
     /usr/games/frotz/        the bundled stories: cluedo.z5 (Mini-Cluedo,
                              public domain) and bookvol.z5 (Book and Volume,
                              CC BY-NC-SA 2.5); README there gives their terms.

NOTES
     The whole story is held in a Memory-Manager block; the writable
     dynamic memory also stays resident for speed.  On a stock 2.8 MHz
     Apple IIgs play is unhurried -- an accelerator is recommended, as
     it is for the system generally.

BUGS
     Timed input is not implemented, so a game's real-time puzzles play
     as untimed.  Sound and graphics (Blorb) are not supported.  A
     single-key prompt reads a whole line and takes its first character.
     A story with more than about 24 KB of dynamic memory runs without
     undo: its undo buffer, two and a half times the dynamic memory, has
     to fit the 64 KB data bank's heap.

AUTHORS
     Frotz by Stefan Jokisch; the terminal interface after Alembic
     Petrofsky's dumb-frotz; the FGX screen, input and story-memory
     layers by the FGX project.  Frotz is distributed under the GNU
     General Public License.
